          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "It is important for you to seek medical advice regarding your recurrent urinary infections. While they are classified as routine urgency, consulting a healthcare provider is essential, especially if your symptoms are frequent or severe.\n\nAccording to the *Infectious Diseases and Urology Society guidance*, recurrent infections may warrant a urine culture to determine resistance patterns and tailor effective management strategies. Management typically involves targeted antibiotic therapy based on culture results, as well as supportive measures such as maintaining proper hydration.\n\nThe recommended approach for treatment includes short courses of antibiotics like nitrofurantoin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, depending on local resistance patterns. Regular follow-up with your clinician is crucial to assess the situation and adjust treatment plans as necessary.\n\nIt is also important to note that different authorities may have varying recommendations. While the *Infectious Diseases and Urology Society* advocates for culture-based antibiotic therapy, others highlight the need for caution regarding antibiotic use and may suggest alternative strategies. Discussing these differences with your healthcare provider can help you navigate the best course of action for managing your recurrent urinary infections effectively.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
